Step 1:
Connect the router to the desktop using a LAN cable. Connect the cable to the appropriate LAN port of the router and the Ethernet port of the desktop. Also, connect the router's power adapter and switch it on.
Step 2:
After successfully connecting the router, reset the router if required. Open a web browser and enter the router's IP address 192.168.0.1 in a new tab. Enter the administrator password, confirm the administrator password, then click Get Started.
Step 3:
Select the appropriate Time Zone, such as Chennai, Kolkata, Mumbai, and New Delhi, and click Next.
Step 4:
Select Static IP as the Connection Type and click Next.
Step 5:
Enter the IP address details according to the local LAN configuration:
•	IP Address: 172.16.46.37
•	Subnet Mask: 255.255.0.0
•	Default Gateway: 172.16.15.254
•	Primary DNS: 8.8.8.8
•	Secondary DNS: 8.8.8.8
Click Next.
Note: To find the IP address on a Windows system, go to Control Panel → Network and Internet → Network and Sharing Center → Change Adapter Settings. Right-click Ethernet, select Properties, choose Internet Protocol Version 4 (TCP/IPv4), and view the IP configuration.
Step 6:
Personalize the Wireless Settings or use the default settings.
Select Smart Connection and enter the following details:
•	Network Name (SSID): CSD
•	Password: 123456789
Click Next.
Step 7:
Select Skip and click Next.
Step 8:
Verify all the configured details and click Next. If prompted, select Skip to continue.
Step 9:
Go to Advanced → Security → Access Control. Enable the Blacklist option and click Add. Select the network device that you want to block and add it to the blacklist.
To allow a previously blocked device, remove the device from the Blacklist.
